#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092063 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c986ab8f-a8a6-5721-bc8d-ba7ec19b95ea" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-727" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = e^{5x +4}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = e^u, \\qquad u=5x+4\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =5\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{df}{dx} &= \\frac{df}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= e^{u} \\cdot 5 \\\\\n&= 5e^{u} \\\\\n&= 5e^{5x + 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092065 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "775dfc95-df31-5198-aeb2-4603b8386957" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-728" :question/problem "Given that $y= 3\\cdot 2^{1 - x}$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 3\\cdot 2^u, \\qquad u=1-x\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 3\\cdot 2^u\\ln(2),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-1\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 2^{u}\\ln (2) \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot 2^{u}\\ln (2) \\\\\n&=-3 \\cdot 2^{1 - x}\\ln (2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092066 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b15a88b5-e2c4-51c1-90d5-031c2a8c23d3" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-729" :question/problem "Given that $y=e^{-x^2}$, calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "First we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = e^u, \\qquad u=-x^2\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-2x\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= e^{u} \\cdot (-2x) \\\\\n&=-2xe^{u} \\\\\n&=-2xe^{-x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54499fe3-6a31-5914-9807-741cd7aca14a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{5}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245388} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d2c4f9-b993-58c3-928b-301fb1fcf34b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{x}^{4}{e}^{{x}^{5}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245389} {:answer/id #uuid "95a12df9-d73c-5ac4-aa6a-b28c5d684278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{5}{e}^{{x}^{5}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245390} {:answer/id #uuid "cd91666e-b21b-5323-af21-9aee63278762" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{5}{e}^{{x}^{5}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245391} {:answer/id #uuid "9a716dc8-cf23-509f-a359-d535efbeb0a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{4}{e}^{{x}^{5}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a716dc8-cf23-509f-a359-d535efbeb0a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}^{4}{e}^{{x}^{5}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245392} :answer-field/id #uuid "06237f78-02b2-5b47-94e1-1ce48c1f62d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245387}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad3e79a7-5a6a-5e4a-b58f-337ea8fbe343" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-107437" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{5}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u={x}^{5}+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (5{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =5{x}^{4}{e}^{{x}^{5}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb162794-e5a5-5cda-9c2a-432d8f3ffe85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-2){e}^{{x}^{2}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245395} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e94af8-91ca-5b0c-b6d7-ac4b719d834c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x+2){e}^{{x}^{2}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245396} {:answer/id #uuid "6e84af23-2ad0-570c-8519-795951156b48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x{e}^{{x}^{2}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245397} {:answer/id #uuid "58cb1f2e-f0a4-5131-acef-9a97dedf3ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{{x}^{2}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245398} {:answer/id #uuid "280a36ef-74b5-5ef4-a77f-a410cb1ddc10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{2}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb162794-e5a5-5cda-9c2a-432d8f3ffe85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x-2){e}^{{x}^{2}-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245395} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd3530a7-f11a-5bc7-aa16-d206e79c4623" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245394}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a322cc4-aad3-5043-bc11-4d63dc62f02a" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-107445" :question/problem "Given that $y={e}^{{x}^{2}-2x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u={x}^{2}-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (2x-2) \\\\  & =(2x-2){e}^{u} \\\\  & =(2x-2){e}^{{x}^{2}-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245400 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1263fa13-a3af-5c6b-9cea-4c1ddcd1e641" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245402} {:answer/id #uuid "88e48b5c-02d0-5789-b3a7-1c1592421bd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245403} {:answer/id #uuid "e41e2e37-bc1d-5137-8df8-dc6c0b4ae3ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245404} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bafd23-09da-5dca-ac5d-8c28e0b7c2ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245405} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2e30a1-b7c9-5df3-bd5a-b9af763f1b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e41e2e37-bc1d-5137-8df8-dc6c0b4ae3ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245404} :answer-field/id #uuid "03a1c15d-7f6a-599c-b0e5-eb1949f001f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245401}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe2b30b6-b282-5eeb-91bd-46c1f612de4f" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-107452" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{{x}^{3}-4x}$ at the point where $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u={x}^{3}-4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-4){e}^{{x}^{3}-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(3{x}^{2}-4){e}^{{x}^{3}-4x}\\,|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =(3(2)^{2}-4){e}^{{2}^{3}-4(2)} \\\\  & =8\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e16268fd-f2f6-5590-936e-367c8df7402f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1}ln⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245409} {:answer/id #uuid "8b838d5d-7a9a-5312-b497-09f1d51087c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1}ln⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245410} {:answer/id #uuid "e582eaeb-e77c-5832-9f9c-e4d10da8abf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1}ln⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245411} {:answer/id #uuid "86da781b-67b7-548a-930d-450588bd2b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1}ln⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245412} {:answer/id #uuid "0b2a06f1-2b56-58b0-a354-adc5cc46e278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1}ln⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86da781b-67b7-548a-930d-450588bd2b39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1}ln⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245412} :answer-field/id #uuid "060f5355-bd73-5340-8a80-a4c7f0cda5f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245408}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f148f6ee-4961-587a-83e8-75698f3f5d2e" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-107459" :question/problem "Given that $y=3\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=3\\cdot {4}^{u},\\,u={x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=3\\cdot {4}^{u}ln⁡4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {4}^{u}ln⁡4\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =6x\\cdot {4}^{{x}^{2}-1}ln⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245414 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d90fd5a9-b1f3-5548-a666-a4f8f072741a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\cdot {6}^{1-4x}ln⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245416} {:answer/id #uuid "065a7ac1-8621-5e79-a54e-43bc66fc881c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24\\cdot {6}^{1-4x}ln⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245417} {:answer/id #uuid "062d0e45-6cea-5d76-9ca2-0b69c9b120a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cdot {6}^{1-4x}ln⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245418} {:answer/id #uuid "96f76ef0-d045-5c1d-a052-5e3bcb6318db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cdot {6}^{1-4x}ln⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245419} {:answer/id #uuid "7c10ff58-e030-5aa7-b5d7-65d611a95b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\cdot {6}^{1-4x}ln⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d90fd5a9-b1f3-5548-a666-a4f8f072741a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\cdot {6}^{1-4x}ln⁡6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245416} :answer-field/id #uuid "5367b2e7-5401-5181-91a9-d1b63476d191" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245415}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d990be1-9c91-5fc6-a2bf-7e557cb860c7" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-107461" :question/problem "If $f(x)={6}^{1-4x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={6}^{u},\\,u=1-4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={6}^{u}ln⁡6,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={6}^{u}ln⁡6\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {6}^{u}ln⁡6 \\\\  & =-4\\cdot {6}^{1-4x}ln⁡6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8f8895d-c35f-5010-9339-3690d53403f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^2\\ln(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8f8895d-c35f-5010-9339-3690d53403f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^2\\ln(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245423} :answer-field/id #uuid "497e3e33-d3a1-5812-8d19-45cd27ffa318" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245422}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "31adc78d-486d-58f1-977f-a0fc1dc4dc68" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-312462" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={5}^{{x}^{3}+1},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,{5}^{{x}^{3}+1}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={5}^{u},\\,u={x}^{3}+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={5}^{u}ln⁡(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={5}^{u}ln⁡(5)\\cdot (3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{u} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}ln⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c35c0b8-2411-5144-9703-7454a558ca0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-3e^{-3x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c35c0b8-2411-5144-9703-7454a558ca0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8-3e^{-3x+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245426} :answer-field/id #uuid "30cab8fe-923d-536c-9351-d3098362f816" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245425}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "404995e1-01a1-5fec-bf0c-884d2a9c9780" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-340496" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=8x+{e}^{-3x+5}.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y={e}^{-3x+5}$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=-3x+5.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3x+5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the sum rule of differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x+{e}^{-3x+5}) \\\\  & =8-3{e}^{-3x+5} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3x+5}+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c80b7c79-cc29-58c6-b60f-df2b2e3e3a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c80b7c79-cc29-58c6-b60f-df2b2e3e3a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245429} :answer-field/id #uuid "576f6ca5-cb08-55f1-86bc-3bdcb32c3baf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245428}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e0a8708-378d-5d66-80bc-b6c370d775c3" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-340497" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y=-4{e}^{2-x}$ at the point where $x=2-ln⁡(3).$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-4{e}^{u},\\,u=2-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-4{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-4{e}^{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4{e}^{u} \\\\  & =4{e}^{2-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=2-ln⁡(3)$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=2-ln⁡(3)} & =4{e}^{2-(2-ln⁡(3))} \\\\  & =4{e}^{ln⁡(3)} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ab3d0c1-7bc1-5747-9f13-f61027a541e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12e^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ab3d0c1-7bc1-5747-9f13-f61027a541e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12e^4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245432} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a875bf6-2743-5e2e-9cfd-a8adca47b189" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245431}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6cca5501-68ec-5237-a389-4b4fb8c8de8e" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-340949" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{4{x}^{3}}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=4{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=12{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (12{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}{e}^{4{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(12{x}^{2}{e}^{4{x}^{3}})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =12(1)^{2}{e}^{4(1)^{3}} \\\\  & =12{e}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fd28a88-626e-53a7-bde1-3395cec5d442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\ln(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245435} {:answer/id #uuid "8c0520ab-7c0b-5598-a9ee-c2ccf4cecb48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\ln(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c0520ab-7c0b-5598-a9ee-c2ccf4cecb48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\ln(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245436} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fb51ef2-f2c1-51af-8e53-40e912184d71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245434}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "451d7bb8-62d7-59f7-8d83-cd6031533c99" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-340969" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={5}^{x-{x}^{3}}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={5}^{u},\\,u=x-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={5}^{u}ln⁡(5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={5}^{u}ln⁡(5)\\cdot (1-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =(1-3{x}^{2}){5}^{x-{x}^{3}}ln⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={((1-3{x}^{2}){5}^{x-{x}^{3}}ln⁡(5))|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =(1-3(1)^{2}){5}^{1-(1)^{3}}ln⁡(5) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {5}^{0}ln⁡(5) \\\\  & =-2ln⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b930837-dfd6-5922-bb79-c8480fc9eabb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245439} {:answer/id #uuid "06d36fb4-8b11-5be3-9aba-f49d2c4831b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245440} {:answer/id #uuid "8abcae28-d559-5e52-8243-4d2cd9c4f8e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245441} {:answer/id #uuid "29e92247-03ea-5dfa-a7f9-1a600fdc8e58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-1}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245442} {:answer/id #uuid "a273d5be-0c27-5579-9562-30cb2c1dee76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{e}^{4x-1}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8abcae28-d559-5e52-8243-4d2cd9c4f8e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{4x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245441} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd533477-a78f-58dd-8434-35e2d2b44216" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245438}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f1bd2ce-06f4-50a6-ab3a-1e29b9357ffa" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48007" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)=-2{e}^{4x-1}+3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=-2{e}^{u}+3,\\,u=4x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=-2{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{u}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-8{e}^{4x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c89d0cac-a243-5f23-9638-1be9577ee65a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{4}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245446} {:answer/id #uuid "852b9da4-a8ca-542b-aba8-397b0a6a3180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8-6{x}^{2}){e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245447} {:answer/id #uuid "00882e0c-621b-54d3-a0a6-35fb824ec8ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{x}^{4}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245448} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ec3275-2843-5b3c-84a7-7720835457a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245449} {:answer/id #uuid "a322bb54-aad8-54c8-9dbd-3f5ef119ac9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a322bb54-aad8-54c8-9dbd-3f5ef119ac9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245450} :answer-field/id #uuid "9de1ba59-468c-5789-b2d0-e54bfee8ad02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245445}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5822257-d381-5490-8999-034f9e1d3660" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48629" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for $f(x)={e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={e}^{u},\\,u=8-2{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-6{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6{x}^{2}{e}^{8-2{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cda33701-cda1-570e-b85d-e13aa2e3d2f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4ex" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245453} {:answer/id #uuid "ca670f03-1065-5401-8b8c-09fc7569ee08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=ex" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245454} {:answer/id #uuid "a4f77739-e4e6-5d69-8262-7b2808f0221a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=20ex" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245455} {:answer/id #uuid "78db1e6f-690b-54a4-90f1-8f1df3a0ed76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5ex" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245456} {:answer/id #uuid "1a0fffd4-74bc-58d7-b483-57ebfa44de89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=20ex\\cdot ln⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4f77739-e4e6-5d69-8262-7b2808f0221a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=20ex" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245455} :answer-field/id #uuid "414233af-99f2-5e00-bea1-764d640b05c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245452}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5aebbdf2-595a-524c-bde1-ffc1725d278e" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48657" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=5{e}^{4x}$ at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{u},\\,u=4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=5{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =5{e}^{u}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =20{e}^{u} \\\\  & =20{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{1}{4}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1/4} & ={(20{e}^{4x})|}_{x=1/4} \\\\  & =20{e}^{4(1/4)} \\\\  & =20e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the $y$-value of the point on the curve where $x={x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & ={(5{e}^{4x})|}_{x=1/4} \\\\  & =5{e}^{4(1/4)} \\\\  & =5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5e & =20e(x-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\ y-5e & =20ex-5e \\\\ y & =20ex\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "048375af-c9c3-596e-b5bf-59742e761c6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245460} {:answer/id #uuid "183bb6a2-7fe6-5490-8bc8-692844a859df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245461} {:answer/id #uuid "44e2ce75-e3fb-5415-8a97-086854a9078c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245462} {:answer/id #uuid "5031a155-b2b6-5774-9ca0-d8af2045f9d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245463} {:answer/id #uuid "31cc3c23-fa04-52c6-9c9d-b75b2cb87b6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44e2ce75-e3fb-5415-8a97-086854a9078c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245462} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d1051a-b535-51a2-8e05-e70fbe39380c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245459}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fca352cd-c420-5e34-a17c-b3f00c5f450b" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48658" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=-3{e}^{5-x}$ at the point where $x=5-ln⁡(2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=-3{e}^{u},\\,u=5-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-3{e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{u}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =3{e}^{u} \\\\  & =3{e}^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=5-ln⁡(2)$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=5-ln⁡(2)} & =3{e}^{5-(5-ln⁡(2))} \\\\  & =3{e}^{ln⁡(2)} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39b9da26-383a-5cf6-bb9f-4549c5132d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7ln⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245467} {:answer/id #uuid "9b123f53-94a3-59a5-b031-69722960b0d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28ln⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245468} {:answer/id #uuid "5bf0c7e6-c687-5f09-ad9d-3e2b6b77e8fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245469} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5dd949-9579-5e18-9ae4-9936263f843e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245470} {:answer/id #uuid "a5cbb9a1-5e36-5d68-8846-b1777adfadd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4ln⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b123f53-94a3-59a5-b031-69722960b0d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28ln⁡(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245468} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8e28b7c-8fe8-53fe-8b78-f4b3064f4084" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245466}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0869377-9313-5d2b-ad1e-f39197afff7e" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-48659" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y={4}^{7x}$ at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={4}^{u},\\,u=7x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={4}^{u}ln⁡4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={4}^{u}ln⁡4\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =7ln⁡4\\cdot {4}^{u} \\\\  & =7ln⁡4\\cdot {4}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=\\frac{1}{7}$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=1/7} & =7ln⁡(4)\\cdot {4}^{7(1/7)} \\\\  & =7ln⁡(4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =28ln⁡(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{1}{7}$ is $28ln⁡(4).$"}]}
